/strong>\u00a0<a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2022\/senate\/ga\/georgia-senate-walker-vs-warnock-7329.html\">Senate\u00a0<\/a>|\u00a0<a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2022\/house\/ga\/georgia_2nd_district_west_vs_bishop-7640.html\">GA-2<\/a><strong><br \/>2020:<\/strong><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/president\/ga\/georgia_trump_vs_biden-6974.html\">President<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/senate\/ga\/georgia_senate_special_election_runoff_loeffler_vs_warnock-7318.html\">Senate<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/senate\/ga\/georgia_senate_runoff_election_perdue_vs_ossoff-7319.html\">Senate<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/house\/ga\/georgia_6th_district_handel_vs_mcbath-7106.html\">GA-6<\/a><strong><br \/>2018:<\/strong><span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/governor\/ga\/georgia_governor_kemp_vs_abrams-6628.html\">Governor<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/ga\/georgia_6th_district_handel_vs_mcbath-6668.html\">GA-6<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/ga\/georgia_7th_district_woodall_vs_bourdeaux-6470.html\">GA-7<\/a><strong><br \/>2017:<span>\u00a0<\/span><\/strong><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2017\/house\/ga\/georgia_6th_district_runoff_election_handel_vs_ossoff-6202.html\">GA-6 (Special)<\/a><strong><br \/>2016:<span>\u00a0<\/span><\/strong><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/president\/ga\/georgia_trump_vs_clinton_vs_johnson_vs_stein-5954.html\">President<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/senate\/ga\/georgia_senate_isakson_vs_barksdale-5955.html\">Senate<\/a><strong><br \/><\/strong><\/p>"},"dataAnalysis":{"title":"Race Analysis","content":"<p>In 2002, Georgia snapped. The state had been among the last in the South to cling to its Democratic roots: Bill Clinton carried it in 1992, and a coalition of African-Americans and rural whites kept Democrats in control of the governor\u2019s mansion, the legislature, and even a Senate seat throughout the decade. All of that changed in 2002, when rural whites revolted against the Democratic establishment, throwing out the Democratic governor and senator and giving Republicans control of the state Senate. Two years later, Republicans captured the state House as well.\u00a0In 2004, Republican Johnny Isakson beat Rep. Denise Majette handily, 58 percent to 40 percent. Democrats enjoyed a reasonably close race in the 2008 general election, but fell badly short in the runoff necessitated when neither Saxby Chambliss nor Jim Martin topped 50 percent.<\/p>\n<p>But demographics were working against the GOP. The Atlanta suburbs accounted for an increasingly large share of the statewide vote, and they swung toward Democrats in the 2010s.\u00a0Joe Biden carried the state by about 12,000 votes in 2020, while Jon Ossoff and Raphael Warnock won both Senate seats in the January 2021 runoffs. But Georgia did not become a blue state so much as a very close one that Democrats win when Republicans nominate poor candidates. Brian Kemp won reelection by seven and a half points in 2022 on the same day that Warnock was forced into another runoff against Herschel Walker. Donald Trump won the state by two points in 2024.<\/p>\n<p>Republicans have a credible but flawed candidate this time. Rep. Mike Collins won a June runoff over Derek Dooley, the more establishment-friendly former University of Tennessee football coach favored by the Kemp operation, after Trump endorsed Collins late in the race. Collins is a trucking company owner with a talent for the sort of online combat the base enjoys, which cuts both ways in a state where the deciding votes are cast in Cobb and Gwinnett counties. Ossoff, meanwhile, has raised an absurd amount of cash, and polls have consistently shown him in the high 40s or low 50s. Collins is stuck in the low 40s. An incumbent at 49 or 50 percent in a midterm environment that is working against his opponent\u2019s party is in good shape, and he should notch a surprisingly easy victory.<\/p>"},"showLineChart":1,"showBarChart":1,"showLineChartFirst":0,"sideCharts":{"charts_on_top":0,"left_chart_race_id":"7319","right_chart_race_id":"4040"},"show_final_results":0,"chart_data":{"chart_id":"7275","chart_axis_x":"1","chart_grid_x":"4","chart_axis_y":"1","chart_grid_y":"10","chart_min":"","chart_max":"","chart_start":"1782968400","chart_end":"","poll_id":"8718","chart_highlight":"#bbbb00","chart_days_extend":"7","chart_height":"360","chart_height_mobile":"360","chart_html5":"1","chart_main_percent":"70","chart_spread_percent":"30","chart_spread_range":"10","chart_name_in_tooltips":"1","chart_spread_force_0_y_axis":"1","chart_y_axis_min":"40","chart_y_axis_max":"54","chart_y_axis_min_m":"40","chart_y_axis_max_m":"54","chart_dates":{"7D":"0","14D":"0","30D":"0","3M":"0","MAX":"1"},"chart_dates_old":[{"period":"DD","count":"14","label":"7D","selected":"0"},{"period":"DD","count":"21","label":"14D","selected":"0"},{"period":"DD","count":"37","label":"30D","selected":"0"},{"period":"MAX","count":"1","label":"MAX","selected":"1"},{"period":"MM","count":"0","label":"3M","selected":"0"}],"chart_dates_mobile":{"7D":"0","14D":"0","30D":"0","3M":"0","MAX":"1"},"chart_dates_mobile_old":[{"period":"DD","count":"14","label":"7D","selected":"0"},{"period":"DD","count":"21","label":"14D","selected":"0"},{"period":"DD","count":"37","label":"30D","selected":"0"},{"period":"MAX","count":"1","label":"MAX","select